<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas-Robert Bugeaud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas-Robert Bugeaud (1784–1849) was a French marshal who, as governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> from 1840 to 1847, led the conquest of the country against Abd al-Qadir. He used mobile columns and a scorched-earth policy of raids, defeated the Moroccan army at Isly in 1844 and encouraged European settlement. He died of cholera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-robert-bugeaud.jpg" alt="Thomas-Robert Bugeaud" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Philippe Larivière · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bugeaud,_Thomas_-_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Robert Bugeaud was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> on 15 October 1784, the youngest of thirteen children of a noble family of Périgord. He ran away from home and for some years worked as an agricultural labourer. At twenty he became a private soldier in the Vélites of the Imperial Guard, took part in the Austerlitz campaign, and was commissioned in 1806.</p><p>He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, Eylau and Pultusk campaigns and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, including the second siege of Zaragossa, and rose to command a regiment under Suchet. He rejoined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a>. After the fall of Napoleon he spent fifteen years without employment, farming and developing Périgord. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830 reopened his career, and in 1831 he was made a brigadier general and elected to the Chamber of Deputies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Although he first disapproved of the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, Bugeaud was sent there and won a victory over Abd al-Qadir at Sikkak on 6 July 1836. He signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tafna">Treaty of Tafna</a> on 30 May 1837; the historian James McDougall argues that its French and Arabic texts differed in a way that shows duplicity on Bugeaud's part.</p><p>Named governor-general in 1840, he put his &quot;flying columns&quot; into effect in 1841, a tactic of raids known as the razzia, built roads, and was made marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1843. At Isly on 14 August 1844 he defeated a Moroccan army and won the title of duke. During his time the number of French colonists grew from 17,000 to 100,000 and French forces from about 60,000 to 109,000 by 1847.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The razzias targeted the agrarian economy and caused widespread famine; one raid on the Beni Zeroual in 1843 killed 150 and took 712 prisoners. In late 1842 about 800 members of the Ouled Riah died in a cave where fires were lit at the entrance, a massacre War Minister Soult tried to cover up but which was leaked to the press and widely condemned. Some historians see Bugeaud as an early practitioner of counter-insurgency. In 1848 he refused to stand for the presidency against Louis Napoleon, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 10 June 1849. In 2024 the Paris city council voted to rename the avenue Bugeaud.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Robert Bugeaud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Thomas-Robert Bugeaud</b>, governor-general from 1840, mobile columns destroyed crops, herds and villages until Abdelkader surrendered in December 1847.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>War resumed in 1839, and <b>Bugeaud</b>'s scorched-earth campaigns destroyed Abdelkader's towns; his mobile camp was captured in 1843.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-abdelkader">Emirate of Abdelkader</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.83,1.26&amp;year=1826" aria-label="Limoges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4580 549 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas-Robert Bugeaud"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5030 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5030 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.83,1.26&amp;year=1826">What was here in 1826? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
